{"parentUuid":"502ef9a2-8e4e-4b02-9c62-d79bba0e3e5e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a053c965da12f63d8","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01U2M7iC6rYskRmLoMGscpXd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KyXnAPNudk7wbWP4fMzTzS","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"SCIP Python pyscipopt 2025 release pyscipopt-nightly"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":212,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4898,"cache_read_input_tokens":24451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4898,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":49,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caoxr1efCN1CC1Avenaa7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a9592465-4aa1-40ec-b89d-177ecbe1b7ee","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:50.528Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"51546902-4c97-4ce9-9797-ba68794e4b49","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a053c965da12f63d8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QgvbtHgR6eLW1vGi6YwSTz","type":"tool_result","content":"# PySAT: Python SAT Solver Toolkit\n\n**PySAT** is a Python toolkit enabling researchers to prototype with Boolean satisfiability (SAT) solvers and related constraint encodings.\n\n## Key Features\n\nThe toolkit integrates multiple state-of-the-art SAT solvers including CaDiCaL, Glucose, Kissat, Lingeling, MapleLCM, Minisat, and others. It also provides numerous cardinality encodings (pairwise, bitwise, sequential counters, sorting networks, totalizer variants) and pseudo-Boolean constraint encodings via PyPBLib integration.\n\n## Core Capabilities\n\nAccording to the repository, the project helps with \"solving problems in NP but also beyond NP,\" supporting applications like MaxSAT solving, minimal unsatisfiability set extraction, and QBF solving.\n\n## Usage Example\n\nThe toolkit represents variables as natural numbers and literals as integers. A basic usage snippet:\n\n```python\nfrom pysat.solvers import Glucose3\ng = Glucose3()\ng.add_clause([-1, 2])\ng.solve()\n```\n\n## Installation & Status\n\n- **Latest Release:** Version 0.1.7.dev15 (December 2021)\n- **Installation:** Via pip: `pip install python-sat[aiger,approxmc,cryptosat,pblib]`\n- **Repository Stats:** 451 stars, 89 forks, 37 open issues\n- **License:** MIT\n\nThe project is written primarily in Python (62.4%) with C++ components (30.4%) for solver implementations."}]},"uuid":"713110d2-87ca-40b7-b758-72d2c009988a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:53.387Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"51546902-4c97-4ce9-9797-ba68794e4b49","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6dda49bf-3558-4dc8-999a-8a80cd9ddfeb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"88dcafdf-a08f-4678-84c0-930bce6a1767","agentId":"a053c965da12f63d8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013fPBP67XL9T6XjeqjgTXRa","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pumpkin Constraint Programming Solver\n\n**Project Status (2025-2026):**\n- 74 GitHub stars, 28 forks\n- Latest release: pumpkin-solver v0.3.0 (February 11, 2026)\n- 594 commits on main branch\n- Active development with 24 open issues and 9 pull requests\n\n**Key Achievement:**\nPumpkin \"won the bronze medal in the fixed search track of the 2025 MiniZinc Challenge,\" demonstrating its competitive performance in constraint solving.\n\n**What It Is:**\nA combinatorial optimization solver developed at TU Delft's ConSol Lab, built entirely in Rust. The system implements lazy clause generation, a constraint programming paradigm. A distinguishing feature is its ability to \"produce a certificate of unsatisfiability,\" enabling formal verification of solutions.\n\n**Technical Capabilities:**\nThe solver supports integer variables and multiple global constraints including cumulative scheduling, disjunctive constraints, element constraints, and arithmetic operations (linear inequalities, multiplication, division, maximum values).\n\n**Accessibility:**\n- Installable via Cargo for Rust projects\n- Command-line interface supporting CNF and FlatZinc formats\n- MiniZinc backend integration available\n- Python bindings provided\n- Dual-licensed under Apache 2.0 and MIT\n\n**Development Quality:**\nWritten in Rust (90.8% of codebase) with emphasis on documentation, pre-commit hooks, and formatting standards. The project includes 15 releases and active academic publications supporting its development."}]},"uuid":"123bc9a5-4619-47ff-b32e-cafe969d3195","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:54.948Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6dda49bf-3558-4dc8-999a-8a80cd9ddfeb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"502ef9a2-8e4e-4b02-9c62-d79bba0e3e5e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"88dcafdf-a08f-4678-84c0-930bce6a1767","agentId":"a053c965da12f63d8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015ZDXLiut5m1JaBmrtSSwR8","type":"tool_result","content":"# RustSAT: A Comprehensive SAT Solving Library\n\n## Overview\nRustSAT is a Rust library designed for boolean satisfiability (SAT) problem solving. The project emphasizes \"ease of use without giving up on performance\" and provides implementations commonly needed in satisfiability solving development.\n\n## Current Status\n- **Stars:** 67\n- **Latest Release:** rustsat-v0.7.5 (January 30, 2026)\n- **Total Releases:** 158\n- **Recent Activity:** Active development in 2025-2026\n\n## Core Components\nThe library consists of multiple specialized crates:\n\n- **rustsat** - Main library with types, traits, encodings, and parsers\n- **rustsat-tools** - Binary utilities for SAT solving tasks\n- **Solver Interfaces:** cadical, kissat, glucose, minisat\n- **rustsat-ipasir** - IPASIR-compliant solver bindings\n- **pigeons** - VeriPB proof logging support\n\n## Key Features\nNotable capabilities include optimization (MaxSAT), multi-objective optimization, compression support, proof logging, and Python bindings available via PyPI.\n\n## Technical Details\n- **Language:** Primarily Rust (92.4%), with some C++ and C\n- **MSRV:** 1.87.0 (maintaining support for code at least one year old)\n- **License:** MIT\n\nThe library includes extensive documentation, examples, and benchmarking tools for comparative analysis with other SAT libraries."}]},"uuid":"eb106cbd-4ddc-40c7-9373-d87158a6aedf","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:55.460Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"502ef9a2-8e4e-4b02-9c62-d79bba0e3e5e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a9592465-4aa1-40ec-b89d-177ecbe1b7ee","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"88dcafdf-a08f-4678-84c0-930bce6a1767","agentId":"a053c965da12f63d8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KyXnAPNudk7wbWP4fMzTzS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SCIP Python pyscipopt 2025 release pyscipopt-nightly\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - scipopt/PySCIPOpt: Python interface for the SCIP Optimization Suite · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scipopt/PySCIPOpt\"},{\"title\":\"PySCIPOpt · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/PySCIPOpt/\"},{\"title\":\"PySCIPOpt Zuse Institute Berlin <scip@zib.de> Nov 29, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://pyscipopt.readthedocs.io/_/downloads/en/stable/pdf/\"},{\"title\":\"PySCIPOpt/INSTALL.md at master · scipopt/PySCIPOpt\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scipopt/PySCIPOpt/blob/master/INSTALL.md\"},{\"title\":\"PySCIPOpt/RELEASE.md at master · scipopt/PySCIPOpt\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scipopt/PySCIPOpt/blob/master/RELEASE.md\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · scipopt/PySCIPOpt\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scipopt/PySCIPOpt/releases\"},{\"title\":\"PySCIPOpt: Mathematical Programming in Python with the SCIP Optimization Suite | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/304919150_PySCIPOpt_Mathematical_Programming_in_Python_with_the_SCIP_Optimization_Suite\"},{\"title\":\"PySCIPOpt/README.md at master · scipopt/PySCIPOpt\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scipopt/PySCIPOpt/blob/master/README.md\"},{\"title\":\"SCIP Doxygen Documentation: Python interface for the SCIP Optimization Suite\",\"url\":\"https://www.scipopt.org/doc-3.2.1/html/PYTHON_INTERFACE.php\"},{\"title\":\"pyscipopt: Python interface and modeling environment for SCIP - Packages and Features\",\"url\":\"https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/spkg/pyscipopt.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about SCIP Python, PySCIPOpt, and 2025 releases:\n\n## Overview of PySCIPOpt\n\nPySCIPOpt provides an interface from Python to the SCIP Optimization Suite. There should be a PySCIPOpt release with every SCIP release, at a minimum.\n\n## Recent Releases\n\nBased on PyPI data, version 6.1.0 was uploaded on February 5, 2026, with wheels available for CPython 3.14.\n\n## Release Process\n\nThe project has documented release procedures. To make a release, the version number is updated according to semantic versioning rules in src/pyscipopt/_version.py and setup.py, and CHANGELOG.md is updated with the new version number.\n\n## Installation\n\nUsers can install PySCIPOpt using pip: pip install pyscipopt\n\n## Regarding \"pyscipopt-nightly\"\n\nWhile my search results show information about PySCIPOpt releases and the general package, they don't contain specific information about a \"pyscipopt-nightly\" build or nightly release channel. The search results focus on official releases and the standard installation methods. If you're looking for nightly build information specifically, you may need to check the project's GitHub Actions workflows or PyPI test server directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c4d33edc-0da5-4b30-ab36-ea17899751ab","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:56.209Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a9592465-4aa1-40ec-b89d-177ecbe1b7ee","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
